# Terraform: Fix Circular Dependency + count/for_each Error

## The Problem

Two common Terraform errors that often appear together:

### Error 1: Circular Dependency
```
Error: Cycle: module.eks.aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.cluster,
module.rds.aws_db_instance.main, module.s3.aws_s3_bucket.assets,
module.eks.aws_iam_role.cluster
```

This typically happens when resources reference each other in a loop — e.g., a security group rule references a security group that references back, or an IAM policy references a resource that depends on the IAM role.

### Error 2: count vs for_each Type Error
```
Error: Invalid count argument
  on modules/s3/main.tf line 12, in resource "aws_s3_bucket" "assets":
  12:   count = var.create_replication ? 1 : 0
The "count" value depends on resource attributes that cannot be determined
until apply, so Terraform cannot predict how many instances will be created.
```

---

## Root Cause: The Broken Code

Here's a representative broken `main.tf` that produces both errors:

```hcl
# ❌ BROKEN - main.tf (root module)

# --- Circular dependency: SG rules reference each other through resources ---
resource "aws_security_group" "eks_nodes" {
  vpc_id = module.vpc.vpc_id
  # ...
}

resource "aws_security_group" "rds" {
  vpc_id = module.vpc.vpc_id
  # ...
}

resource "aws_security_group_rule" "rds_from_eks" {
  security_group_id        = aws_security_group.rds.id
  source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.eks_nodes.id
  type                     = "ingress"
  from_port                = 5432
  to_port                  = 5432
  protocol                 = "tcp"
}

resource "aws_security_group_rule" "eks_from_rds" {
  # ❌ CIRCULAR: eks_nodes SG now depends on rds SG, which depends on eks_nodes SG
  security_group_id        = aws_security_group.eks_nodes.id
  source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.rds.id
  type                     = "ingress"
  from_port                = 1025
  to_port                  = 65535
  protocol                 = "tcp"
}

# --- count error: using a resource attribute in count ---
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "replica" {
  # ❌ ERROR: aws_s3_bucket.assets.arn is not known until apply
  count = aws_s3_bucket.assets.arn != "" ? 1 : 0

  bucket   = "${var.app_name}-assets-replica-${var.environment}"
  provider = aws.west
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "assets" {
  bucket = "${var.app_name}-assets-${var.environment}"
}

# --- Another count error: conditional on computed value ---
resource "aws_db_instance" "main" {
  # ❌ ERROR: depends on data source that requires apply
  count = data.aws_caller_identity.current.account_id != "" ? 1 : 0
  # ...
}
```

---

## The Fix

### Strategy

| Problem | Fix |
|---|---|
| Circular SG dependency | Use standalone `aws_security_group_rule` resources (not inline), and only create rules in **one direction** |
| `count` on computed values | Use `for_each` with a variable-driven map, or use a variable flag instead of a resource attribute |
| Conditional resources | Drive conditionals from `var.*` (known at plan time), never from resource attributes |

### Fixed File Structure

```
terraform/
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
├── dev.tfvars
├── prod.tfvars
├── modules/
│   ├── networking/
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│   ├── eks/
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│   ├── s3/
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│   ├── rds/
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│   ├── iam/
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│   ├── cdn/
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│   └── secrets/
│       ├── main.tf
│       ├── variables.tf
│       └── outputs.tf
```

---

### `variables.tf`

```hcl
variable "environment" {
  description = "Deployment environment"
  type        = string
  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "Environment must be dev or prod."
  }
}

variable "region" {
  description = "Primary AWS region"
  type        = string
}

variable "replica_region" {
  description = "Cross-region replication target (prod only)"
  type        = string
  default     = "us-west-2"
}

variable "app_name" {
  type    = string
  default = "webapp"
}

# --- EKS ---
variable "eks_node_count" {
  type = number
}

variable "eks_instance_type" {
  type = string
}

# --- RDS ---
variable "rds_instance_class" {
  type = string
}

variable "rds_multi_az" {
  type    = bool
  default = false
}

variable "db_password" {
  description = "RDS master password (supply via -var or SOPS)"
  type        = string
  sensitive   = true
}

# --- Feature flags (plan-time known, safe for count/for_each) ---
variable "enable_replication" {
  description = "Enable S3 cross-region replication (prod)"
  type        = bool
  default     = false
}

variable "enable_cloudfront" {
  description = "Use CloudFront + ACM instead of internal ALB (prod)"
  type        = bool
  default     = false
}

variable "rds_storage_encrypted" {
  type    = bool
  default = true
}
```

---

### `dev.tfvars`

```hcl
environment         = "dev"
region              = "us-east-1"
eks_node_count      = 2
eks_instance_type   = "t3.medium"
rds_instance_class  = "db.t3.micro"
rds_multi_az        = false
enable_replication  = false
enable_cloudfront   = false
# db_password supplied via: terraform apply -var-file=dev.tfvars -var="db_password=..."
```

### `prod.tfvars`

```hcl
environment         = "prod"
region              = "us-east-1"
replica_region      = "us-west-2"
eks_node_count      = 4
eks_instance_type   = "t3.large"
rds_instance_class  = "db.t3.medium"
rds_multi_az        = true
enable_replication  = true
enable_cloudfront   = true
rds_storage_encrypted = true
```

---

### `main.tf` (root module) — Fixed

```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.5"
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
    sops = {
      source  = "carlpett/sops"
      version = "~> 1.0"
    }
  }

  backend "s3" {
    # State key includes workspace for env isolation
    # terraform workspace select dev|prod
    bucket         = "webapp-terraform-state"
    key            = "env:/${terraform.workspace}/webapp/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    encrypt        = true
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-locks"
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = var.region

  default_tags {
    tags = {
      Environment = var.environment
      ManagedBy   = "terraform"
      Project     = var.app_name
    }
  }
}

# Second provider for cross-region replication
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "replica"
  region = var.replica_region

  default_tags {
    tags = {
      Environment = var.environment
      ManagedBy   = "terraform"
      Project     = var.app_name
    }
  }
}

# ============================================================
# Networking (VPC, subnets, security groups)
# ============================================================
module "networking" {
  source = "./modules/networking"

  environment = var.environment
  app_name    = var.app_name
  region      = var.region
}

# ============================================================
# EKS Cluster
# ============================================================
module "eks" {
  source = "./modules/eks"

  environment       = var.environment
  app_name          = var.app_name
  node_count        = var.eks_node_count
  instance_type     = var.eks_instance_type
  vpc_id            = module.networking.vpc_id
  private_subnet_ids = module.networking.private_subnet_ids
  node_sg_id        = module.networking.eks_nodes_sg_id
}

# ============================================================
# S3 Bucket(s)
# FIX: Use for_each with a variable-driven map, not count
# on a computed resource attribute.
# ============================================================
module "s3" {
  source = "./modules/s3"

  providers = {
    aws         = aws
    aws.replica = aws.replica
  }

  environment        = var.environment
  app_name           = var.app_name
  enable_replication = var.enable_replication  # ✅ variable, not resource attr
  replica_region     = var.replica_region
  eks_node_role_arn  = module.eks.node_role_arn
}

# ============================================================
# RDS PostgreSQL
# ============================================================
module "rds" {
  source = "./modules/rds"

  environment         = var.environment
  app_name            = var.app_name
  instance_class      = var.rds_instance_class
  multi_az            = var.rds_multi_az
  storage_encrypted   = var.rds_storage_encrypted
  vpc_id              = module.networking.vpc_id
  db_subnet_group_name = module.networking.db_subnet_group_name
  rds_sg_id           = module.networking.rds_sg_id
  db_password         = var.db_password
}

# ============================================================
# IAM (service account / OIDC)
# ============================================================
module "iam" {
  source = "./modules/iam"

  environment       = var.environment
  app_name          = var.app_name
  oidc_provider_arn = module.eks.oidc_provider_arn
  oidc_provider_url = module.eks.oidc_provider_url
  s3_bucket_arn     = module.s3.bucket_arn
}

# ============================================================
# HTTPS Exposure (ALB for dev, CloudFront for prod)
# ============================================================
module "cdn" {
  source = "./modules/cdn"

  environment       = var.environment
  app_name          = var.app_name
  enable_cloudfront = var.enable_cloudfront  # ✅ variable flag
  vpc_id            = module.networking.vpc_id
  public_subnet_ids = module.networking.public_subnet_ids
  s3_bucket_domain  = module.s3.bucket_regional_domain_name
}

# ============================================================
# Secrets (SOPS-encrypted DB connection string)
# ============================================================
module "secrets" {
  source = "./modules/secrets"

  environment = var.environment
  app_name    = var.app_name
  db_host     = module.rds.endpoint
  db_password = var.db_password
}
```

---

### `modules/networking/main.tf` — Fixed Security Groups

The circular dependency fix: **security groups are standalone, rules are one-directional**.

```hcl
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "app_name"    { type = string }
variable "region"      { type = string }

data "aws_availability_zones" "available" {
  state = "available"
}

locals {
  azs = slice(data.aws_availability_zones.available.names, 0, 3)
}

module "vpc" {
  source  = "terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws"
  version = "~> 5.0"

  name = "${var.app_name}-${var.environment}"
  cidr = "10.0.0.0/16"

  azs              = local.azs
  private_subnets  = ["10.0.1.0/24", "10.0.2.0/24", "10.0.3.0/24"]
  public_subnets   = ["10.0.101.0/24", "10.0.102.0/24", "10.0.103.0/24"]
  database_subnets = ["10.0.201.0/24", "10.0.202.0/24", "10.0.203.0/24"]

  enable_nat_gateway   = true
  single_nat_gateway   = var.environment == "dev" ? true : false
  enable_dns_hostnames = true

  create_database_subnet_group = true
}

# ============================================================
# FIX: Security groups created WITHOUT inline rules that
# reference each other. Rules are separate resources below,
# and only go in ONE direction (EKS → RDS, not RDS → EKS).
# ============================================================

resource "aws_security_group" "eks_nodes" {
  name_prefix = "${var.app_name}-eks-nodes-${var.environment}-"
  vpc_id      = module.vpc.vpc_id

  # No inline ingress/egress rules that reference other SGs

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
    description = "Allow all outbound"
  }

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.app_name}-eks-nodes-${var.environment}"
  }

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}

resource "aws_security_group" "rds" {
  name_prefix = "${var.app_name}-rds-${var.environment}-"
  vpc_id      = module.vpc.vpc_id

  # No inline rules referencing eks_nodes SG

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.app_name}-rds-${var.environment}"
  }

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}

# ✅ FIX: One-directional rule only — RDS accepts from EKS nodes
# This is a standalone resource, so it doesn't create a cycle.
resource "aws_security_group_rule" "rds_from_eks" {
  type                     = "ingress"
  from_port                = 5432
  to_port                  = 5432
  protocol                 = "tcp"
  security_group_id        = aws_security_group.rds.id
  source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.eks_nodes.id
  description              = "PostgreSQL from EKS nodes"
}

# ❌ REMOVED: The reverse rule (eks_from_rds) that caused the cycle.
# EKS nodes don't need inbound from RDS. If they did, we'd add it
# as a standalone rule here — but only if truly needed.

# ALB security group
resource "aws_security_group" "alb" {
  name_prefix = "${var.app_name}-alb-${var.environment}-"
  vpc_id      = module.vpc.vpc_id

  ingress {
    from_port   = 443
    to_port     = 443
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
    description = "HTTPS"
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.app_name}-alb-${var.environment}"
  }

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}

# ============================================================
# Outputs
# ============================================================
output "vpc_id"              { value = module.vpc.vpc_id }
output "private_subnet_ids"  { value = module.vpc.private_subnets }
output "public_subnet_ids"   { value = module.vpc.public_subnets }
output "db_subnet_group_name" { value = module.vpc.database_subnet_group_name }
output "eks_nodes_sg_id"     { value = aws_security_group.eks_nodes.id }
output "rds_sg_id"           { value = aws_security_group.rds.id }
output "alb_sg_id"           { value = aws_security_group.alb.id }
```

---

### `modules/eks/main.tf`

```hcl
variable "environment"        { type = string }
variable "app_name"           { type = string }
variable "node_count"         { type = number }
variable "instance_type"      { type = string }
variable "vpc_id"             { type = string }
variable "private_subnet_ids" { type = list(string) }
variable "node_sg_id"         { type = string }

module "eks" {
  source  = "terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws"
  version = "~> 20.0"

  cluster_name    = "${var.app_name}-${var.environment}"
  cluster_version = "1.29"

  vpc_id     = var.vpc_id
  subnet_ids = var.private_subnet_ids

  cluster_endpoint_public_access = var.environment == "dev" ? true : false

  # Managed node groups per spec
  eks_managed_node_groups = {
    main = {
      desired_size  = var.node_count
      min_size      = var.node_count
      max_size      = var.node_count + 2
      instance_types = [var.instance_type]

      vpc_security_group_ids = [var.node_sg_id]

      labels = {
        Environment = var.environment
      }
    }
  }

  # OIDC provider for IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA)
  enable_irsa = true

  tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
  }
}

output "cluster_name"      { value = module.eks.cluster_name }
output "cluster_endpoint"  { value = module.eks.cluster_endpoint }
output "node_role_arn"     { value = module.eks.eks_managed_node_groups["main"].iam_role_arn }
output "oidc_provider_arn" { value = module.eks.oidc_provider_arn }
output "oidc_provider_url" { value = module.eks.cluster_oidc_issuer_url }
```

